  "releaseNotes": "v0.8.1: counts are advisory and can no longer affect reads. Every CountAsync (GET /api/v1/Customer/info/count, /api/v1/Product/info/count, and the dkERP table change counts behind the vendor and group counts: APVEND, ARGROUPS, INGROUPS, APGROUPS) and Table.GetChangeSummaryAsync now run in a resilience pipeline of their own: one attempt, never retried, bounded by the new DkPlusResilienceOptions.CountTimeout (default 15 seconds, validated: greater than 10 ms and less than 1 day; set it as options.Resilience.CountTimeout on DkPlusClientOptions or DkPlusMultiTenantOptions), and never sampled by the circuit breaker that guards reads. Before, a count shared the read pipeline: a count throttled with HTTP 429 was retried three times and its four failed attempts could open the per-authority read circuit, so the mandatory read that followed was rejected with DkPlusCircuitOpenException. Now a throttled count fails at once with DkPlusRateLimitException and a slow one with DkPlusTimeoutException carrying the count timeout. CountTimeout is one end-to-end deadline that covers the send, the success body and its deserialization, and the error body, so a server that sends headers and then stalls or drips the body (a 429 body included) cannot hold a count; caller cancellation still surfaces as OperationCanceledException. The count that sizes an unlimited Table.GetRecordsAsync read is part of that read and keeps the read pipeline. Counts keep the same transport, endpoint policy, per-request credentials, correlation ID, response size bound, and error mapping. This applies to every client kind: AddDkPlusClient, AddDkPlus (contextual), AddScopedDkPlusClient, and DkPlusClient.Create; a client built with the public constructor around a caller-owned HttpClient uses that client's pipeline. Public API: one added property, DkPlusResilienceOptions.CountTimeout; nothing else changed.",
